#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 480.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186143679} :activity/id #uuid "23fe56b8-25f5-5fd0-a996-2dbf45b14830" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049357 :topic/id #uuid "ee85e121-36ea-5d80-b70f-6a651e0e2e5f" :topic/math-academy-id 2377} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186143679 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186144069 :tutorial/content "To divide a whole number by a *unit* fraction, we multiply the whole number by the fraction's denominator. \n\nFor example,\n\n$$\n{\\color{red}{2}}\\div \\dfrac 1{\\color{blue}{3}} = {\\color{red}{2}}\\times {\\color{blue}{3}} = 6.\n$$\n\nNow, note the following:\n\n- ${\\color{blue}{3}}$ is the *reciprocal* of $\\dfrac1{\\color{blue}{3}}.$\n\n- So, to calculate ${\\color{red}{2}}\\div \\dfrac 1{\\color{blue}{3}},$ we multiplied $\\color{red}2$ by the *reciprocal* of $\\dfrac1{\\color{blue}{3}}.$\n\nThinking about division in terms of reciprocals will help us later when we want to divide whole numbers by non-unit fractions." :tutorial/id #uuid "62afd24d-2d5f-55da-8bca-04a25ea88826" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8563 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "68b6f91a-e88c-5eec-8094-016d0c8d9fc3" :step/math-academy-id 136178 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186143680}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186143680 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186142805 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186142590 :question/id #uuid "58c82929-55bc-5fcf-b4f1-d685a065efe4" :question/math-academy-id "e-13701"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8e58a7d0-dfe1-52dd-9de0-f598aca658df" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049356} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049526}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186143238} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186143328} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186432144} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186432145} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186432147} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186432148} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186432149}] :knowledge-point/title "Dividing Whole Numbers by Unit Fractions"} :step/id #uuid "70afaa12-7c0f-5182-9a03-1ad778d72893" :step/math-academy-id 136179 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186143681}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186143681 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186144070 :tutorial/content "When dividing a whole number by a non-unit fraction, we can still use the same technique of multiplying the whole number by the reciprocal of the fraction. \n\nThe only difference is that, in order to perform the multiplication, we must convert the whole number into an improper fraction.\n\nTo illustrate, let's compute $3\\div \\dfrac 2 9 .$\n\nFirst, we write $3$ as an improper fraction:\n\n$$\n3=\\dfrac{3}{1}\n$$\n\nNext, we note that the reciprocal of $\\dfrac{\\color{blue}2}{\\color{red}9}$ is $\\dfrac{\\color{red}9}{\\color{blue}2}  .$ \n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n3\\div \\dfrac 2 9 = \n\n\\dfrac 3 1 \\div \\dfrac{\\color{blue}2}{\\color{red}9} = \\dfrac 3 1 \\times  \\dfrac{\\color{red}9}{\\color{blue}2}.\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we multiply our fractions. \n\n$$\n\\dfrac 3 {1} \\times \\dfrac{9}{2} =  \\dfrac {3\\times 9} {1\\times 2} = \\dfrac{27}{2}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "b398eb3e-55b7-5e25-b21e-2625b0b68d7b" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8564 :tutorial/title "Dividing Whole Numbers by Non-Unit Fractions"} :step/id #uuid "70a09d80-1df8-57cc-a056-1f19852b057f" :step/math-academy-id 136182 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186143682}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186143682 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186142806 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186142593 :question/id #uuid "e86212dc-fc03-58a2-9a37-758e85a18073" :question/math-academy-id "e-13708"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "dd40bd52-0088-5405-b03a-71aa22a2fe49" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049253} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050249}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186143176} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186143305} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186432137} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186432138} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186432140} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186432141} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186432152}] :knowledge-point/title "Dividing Whole Numbers by Fractions"} :step/id #uuid "3540593d-e892-519f-8b52-fde9d5905152" :step/math-academy-id 136183 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186143683}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186143683 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186144097 :tutorial/content "Let's find the value of $8\\div \\dfrac 4 5.$\n\nDividing a whole number by a fraction is equivalent to multiplying the whole number by the reciprocal of the fraction.\n\nFirst, we write $8$ as an improper fraction:\n\n$$\n8=\\dfrac{8}{1}\n$$\n\nNext, we note that the reciprocal of $\\dfrac{\\color{blue}4}{\\color{red}5}$ is $\\dfrac{\\color{red}5}{\\color{blue}4}.$ \n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n8\\div \\dfrac 4 5 = \n\\dfrac 8 1 \\div \\dfrac{\\color{blue}4}{\\color{red}5} = \\dfrac 8 1 \\times  \\dfrac{\\color{red}5}{\\color{blue}4}.\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we multiply our fractions. \n\n$$\n\\dfrac 8 {1} \\times \\dfrac{5}{4} =  \\dfrac {8\\times 5} {1\\times 4} = \\dfrac{40}{4}\n$$\n\nFinally, we simplify:\n\n$$\n40 \\div 4 = 10.\n$$\n\nSometimes, we may wish to use a fraction multiplication strategy to simplify the problem *before* multiplying the fractions. Let's see an example." :tutorial/id #uuid "0c0a762b-e74c-5f6a-8636-574d1a556b4c" :tutorial/math-academy-id 9008 :tutorial/title "Simplifying a Multiplication Problem"} :step/id #uuid "434249a0-0335-57db-8f35-abae92a17cf5" :step/math-academy-id 136186 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186143684}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186143684 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186142807 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186142592 :question/id #uuid "42efe817-2f21-57a9-9cfe-5973ac89f1f2" :question/math-academy-id "e-13706"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d2ddc37a-2ee1-50db-adf7-9b15360f8227" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050927}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186143175} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186143237} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186432136} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186432142} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186432146} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186432150}] :knowledge-point/title "Applying a Strategy (Result Is a Whole Number)"} :step/id #uuid "abcf5e8b-b4c8-52e8-9ddb-5a2ad79f5cf0" :step/math-academy-id 136187 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186143685}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186143685 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186142808 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186142591 :question/id #uuid "906c6d3e-4f1a-58e0-8f21-128bfc754d47" :question/math-academy-id "e-13703"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d6aef2b2-fa3d-5c58-9868-67513adfef9b" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186143239} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186143240} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186432139} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186432143} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186432151}] :knowledge-point/title "Applying a Strategy (Result Is a Fraction or Mixed Number)"} :step/id #uuid "5a44dd4c-50c5-567f-bb2d-ee5809d43d09" :step/math-academy-id 136190}] :activity/title "Dividing Whole Numbers by Fractions" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186143012}]}
